1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,014
In a ring game, kids spin
in a line, holding hands.

2
00:00:04,014 --> 00:00:05,319
The end kid runs.

3
00:00:05,319 --> 00:00:07,120
The centre kid just turns.

4
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:11,058
Swing a cricket bat, and the tip
moves faster than the handle.

5
00:00:11,508 --> 00:00:14,041
At the fair, Diya rides a merry-go-round.

6
00:00:14,041 --> 00:00:15,959
Her horse is near the centre.

7
00:00:15,959 --> 00:00:17,737
Her friend is at the edge.

8
00:00:18,087 --> 00:00:19,301
They turn together.

9
00:00:19,301 --> 00:00:22,828
Yet her friend rushes through
the air, and Diya just drifts.

10
00:00:22,828 --> 00:00:23,728
How?

11
00:00:23,776 --> 00:00:27,927
The answer links two speeds:
angular and linear velocity.

12
00:00:28,527 --> 00:00:33,378
Angular velocity, omega, is the angle
turned each second, in radians per

13
00:00:33,378 --> 00:00:34,278
second.

14
00:00:34,413 --> 00:00:38,660
In a rigid body, every point turns
the same angle in the same time.

15
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:40,634
So all points share one omega.

16
00:00:41,034 --> 00:00:43,343
Omega is a vector along the axis.

17
00:00:43,343 --> 00:00:47,693
Curl your right fingers with the
turn, and your thumb gives omega.

18
00:00:48,093 --> 00:00:53,231
Linear velocity, v, is how fast one
point moves along its circle, in metres

19
00:00:53,231 --> 00:00:54,188
per second.

20
00:00:54,788 --> 00:00:56,477
To the motion notebook.

21
00:00:56,477 --> 00:01:00,493
The merry-go-round turns at zero
point five radians per second.

22
00:01:00,493 --> 00:01:01,759
Omega points up.

23
00:01:02,109 --> 00:01:04,936
The inner horse is one
metre from the pole.

24
00:01:04,936 --> 00:01:07,907
It moves at zero point
five metres per second.

25
00:01:08,307 --> 00:01:10,744
The outer horse is three metres out.

26
00:01:10,744 --> 00:01:13,744
Same omega, but it
moves at one point five.

27
00:01:13,744 --> 00:01:15,106
Three times faster.

28
00:01:15,506 --> 00:01:19,694
Now a marker walks out along the
floor, from the pole to the edge.

29
00:01:20,094 --> 00:01:23,450
On the graph, its speed
rises in a straight line.

30
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:26,894
Double the distance from
the axis, double the speed.

31
00:01:27,394 --> 00:01:28,423
Slow-mo replay.

32
00:01:28,423 --> 00:01:29,999
Freeze the outer horse.

33
00:01:30,349 --> 00:01:35,438
Draw r from a point O, low on the pole,
to the horse: five metres, at angle

34
00:01:35,438 --> 00:01:36,793
theta to the pole.

35
00:01:37,193 --> 00:01:40,286
Its distance from the
axis is r sine theta.

36
00:01:40,286 --> 00:01:42,232
Five times zero point six.

37
00:01:42,232 --> 00:01:43,305
Three metres.

38
00:01:43,706 --> 00:01:48,387
And v is at right angles to both
omega and r, along the circle.

39
00:01:48,387 --> 00:01:50,447
So v equals omega cross r.

40
00:01:51,047 --> 00:01:52,011
Now you try.

41
00:01:52,011 --> 00:01:54,089
A point sits right on the pole.

42
00:01:54,089 --> 00:01:55,633
How fast does it move?

43
00:01:55,633 --> 00:01:56,831
Pause and decide.

44
00:01:59,931 --> 00:02:00,831
Zero!

45
00:02:00,475 --> 00:02:04,340
It is on the axis, so its
distance from the axis is zero.

46
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:06,195
It just turns on the spot.

47
00:02:06,795 --> 00:02:09,393
Now let's derive it, one step at a time.

48
00:02:09,743 --> 00:02:10,643
Step one.

49
00:02:10,614 --> 00:02:15,861
On a circle, arc length is radius
times angle, with the angle in radians.

50
00:02:16,611 --> 00:02:17,511
Step two.

51
00:02:17,482 --> 00:02:22,676
Each second it turns omega radians,
so the point moves omega times r perp.

52
00:02:22,676 --> 00:02:24,429
v equals omega r perp.

53
00:02:25,179 --> 00:02:26,132
Step three.

54
00:02:26,132 --> 00:02:30,372
With r from any point on the
axis, r perp is r sine theta.

55
00:02:30,372 --> 00:02:32,467
So v is omega r sine theta.

56
00:02:33,217 --> 00:02:34,117
Step four.

57
00:02:34,084 --> 00:02:36,606
That is the size of a cross product.

58
00:02:36,606 --> 00:02:39,317
With direction, v equals omega cross r.

59
00:02:40,067 --> 00:02:41,101
Remember this.

60
00:02:41,101 --> 00:02:43,034
Every point shares one omega.

61
00:02:43,034 --> 00:02:46,367
Its speed is omega times
its distance from the axis.

62
00:02:47,067 --> 00:02:49,888
Back to the two horses, link by link.

63
00:02:50,238 --> 00:02:54,823
Both horses finish a turn in the same
time, about twelve point six seconds.

64
00:02:55,223 --> 00:02:58,474
In that turn, each goes
once round its own circle.

65
00:02:58,474 --> 00:03:00,943
The outer circle is three times longer.

66
00:03:01,343 --> 00:03:05,530
Three times the distance in the
same time: three times the speed.

67
00:03:06,130 --> 00:03:07,291
Side by side.

68
00:03:07,291 --> 00:03:12,429
Inner horse: one metre out, omega
zero point five, v zero point five.

69
00:03:12,829 --> 00:03:17,762
Outer horse: three metres out, the
same omega, but v is one point five.

70
00:03:18,362 --> 00:03:19,312
Now you try.

71
00:03:19,312 --> 00:03:21,119
The Earth turns once a day.

72
00:03:21,119 --> 00:03:24,475
Who moves faster: someone
in Bengaluru, or in London?

73
00:03:24,475 --> 00:03:25,655
Pause and decide.

74
00:03:28,855 --> 00:03:29,755
Bengaluru!

75
00:03:29,609 --> 00:03:30,509
Same omega.

76
00:03:30,465 --> 00:03:34,541
But Bengaluru is nearer the equator,
so it is farther from the Earth's

77
00:03:34,541 --> 00:03:35,441
axis.

78
00:03:35,712 --> 00:03:37,402
Now, one worked example.

79
00:03:37,752 --> 00:03:42,100
The Earth's radius is six point three
seven times ten to the six metres.

80
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:46,570
Find the speed on the equator, and
in Bengaluru, thirteen degrees north.

81
00:03:47,070 --> 00:03:51,760
One turn a day: omega is two pi over
eighty six thousand four hundred.

82
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:55,470
About seven point two seven
times ten to the minus five.

83
00:03:56,220 --> 00:03:59,307
On the equator, r perp is the full radius.

84
00:03:59,307 --> 00:04:03,240
v is about four hundred and
sixty three metres per second.

85
00:04:03,990 --> 00:04:09,024
In Bengaluru, r perp is R cos thirteen,
about six point two one times ten

86
00:04:09,024 --> 00:04:10,482
to the six metres.

87
00:04:11,232 --> 00:04:15,996
So v is about four hundred and fifty
one metres per second, and we never

88
00:04:15,996 --> 00:04:16,896
feel it.

89
00:04:17,435 --> 00:04:18,897
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:04:19,247 --> 00:04:24,229
Many students use the full distance
r from the origin: v equals omega r.

91
00:04:24,629 --> 00:04:27,349
But only the distance
from the axis counts.

92
00:04:27,349 --> 00:04:30,266
For our horse, omega r
gives two point five.

93
00:04:30,266 --> 00:04:32,164
The truth is one point five.

94
00:04:32,814 --> 00:04:34,389
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:34,389 --> 00:04:39,504
v equals omega cross r, points on
wheels and on the Earth, and rolling.

96
00:04:40,104 --> 00:04:41,322
Exam level now.

97
00:04:41,322 --> 00:04:46,047
Omega is four k radians per second,
about an axis through the origin.

98
00:04:46,047 --> 00:04:50,792
Find the speed of the point three
i plus four j plus twelve k metres.

99
00:04:50,792 --> 00:04:52,061
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:57,461 --> 00:04:58,840
The answer is B.

101
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:00,700
Twenty metres per second.

102
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:02,968
v is omega cross r.

103
00:05:02,968 --> 00:05:05,772
Four k cross three i is twelve j.

104
00:05:05,772 --> 00:05:09,001
Four k cross four j is minus sixteen i.

105
00:05:09,501 --> 00:05:12,466
So v is minus sixteen i plus twelve j.

106
00:05:12,466 --> 00:05:15,023
Its size is root of four hundred.

107
00:05:15,023 --> 00:05:15,923
Twenty.

108
00:05:16,251 --> 00:05:20,026
Check: r perp is five, and
four times five is twenty.

109
00:05:20,026 --> 00:05:22,043
The twelve k does not count.

110
00:05:22,644 --> 00:05:24,184
Here is your recap.

111
00:05:24,184 --> 00:05:25,766
Screenshot this card.

112
00:05:28,966 --> 00:05:30,905
Can you answer these without help?

113
00:05:31,305 --> 00:05:32,205
One.

114
00:05:31,736 --> 00:05:34,965
Why do all points of a turning
body share the same omega?

115
00:05:35,865 --> 00:05:36,765
Two.

116
00:05:36,279 --> 00:05:38,462
Why does a point on the axis not move?

117
00:05:39,362 --> 00:05:40,262
Three.

118
00:05:40,012 --> 00:05:43,870
Why use r sine theta, not
r, in v equals omega r?

119
00:05:44,770 --> 00:05:49,014
Next time: a ceiling fan takes a
few seconds to reach full speed.

120
00:05:49,014 --> 00:05:51,668
How many turns does it make on the way?

121
00:05:51,668 --> 00:05:53,044
Angular acceleration.
